# Age-Structured Network SIR Model for Disease Transmission Analysis

## Overview

This project implements an age-structured discrete-time SIR epidemic model on contact networks to analyze disease transmission dynamics across different countries with varying demographic structures.

## Project Structure

### Core Scripts

- `network_contruct.py` - Constructs contact networks from population and contact matrix data
- `age_structured_discrete_SIR_model.py` - Main SIR simulation engine (baseline scenario)
- `age_structured_discrete_SIR_model_no_school.py` - SIR model with school closure intervention
- `age_structured_discrete_SIR_model_no_work.py` - SIR model with workplace closure intervention
- `final_size_calculator.py` - Calculates theoretical final epidemic size
- `age_group_ranking_analysis.py` - Ranks countries by age-group-specific infection rates

### Visualization Folders

- `Fig2-chord-heatmap/` - Network structure visualization (chord diagrams and heatmaps)
- `fig3,fig4/` - Network degree distribution analysis
- `Fig5,Fig6/` - R0 and final size relationship analysis
- `Fig8/` - Age group comparison across countries
- `Fig9/` - Intervention impact on network structure
- `Fig11/` - No-school intervention results
- `Fig12/` - No-work intervention results

### Data Directories

- `gexf_networks/` - Generated contact networks (baseline)
- `gexf_networks_no_school/` - Networks with school connections removed
- `gexf_networks_no_work/` - Networks with workplace connections removed
- `population fraction/` - Raw population distribution data (Excel files)
- `population_fraction_processed_csv/` - Processed population fractions (CSV)
- `processed_matrices/` - Symmetrized contact matrices (Pickle format)
- `results_by_country/` - Final size calculation results for all countries
- `Gillespie_SIR_Results*/` - SIR simulation outputs for different scenarios


Age exposure data are from reference .
K. Prem, A. R. Cook, and M. Jit, “Projecting social contact matrices in 152 countries using contact surveys and demographic data,” PLoS Comput Biol, vol. 13, no. 9, p. e1005697, Sep. 2017, doi: 10.1371/journal.pcbi.1005697.
J. Mossong et al., “Social Contacts and Mixing Patterns Relevant to the Spread of Infectious Diseases,” PLoS Med, vol. 5, no. 3, p. e74, Mar. 2008, doi: 10.1371/journal.pmed.0050074.

Age exposure data are from reference .
“Population Pyramids of the World from 1950 to 2100,” PopulationPyramid.net. Accessed: Feb. 20, 2026. [Online]. Available: https://www.populationpyramid.net/             world/2026/
